{"id": "h-21", "question": "Will the policy pay for my dental implants?", "strata": "clause_tension", "authored_by": "human", "expected_behaviour": "answer", "concept_id": "exclusion.permanent", "ground_truth_spans": [{"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 35, "clause_id": "excl.32"}, {"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 16, "clause_id": "17"}], "must_not_cite": ["star-comprehensive-2025#def.day_care_treatment", "star-comprehensive-2025#def.day_care_centre"], "as_of_date": "2026-09-04", "notes": "THREE-WAY TENSION, all inside one current document. p35 excl.32: 'Dental treatment or surgery unless necessitated due to accidental injuries and requiring hospitalization (Dental implants are not payable) (except to the extent covered under Section II.17) - Code Excl 32'. p16 clause 17 'Out-patient Dental and Ophthalmic Treatment' has two limbs: 'acute treatment to a natural tooth or teeth' - an implant is not a natural tooth, so this limb excludes it - OR 'the services and supplies provided by a licensed dentist', which an implant plainly is, up to a table limit per block of 3 continuous years (Rs 5,000 / 10,000 / 15,000 by sum insured). So the flat parenthetical denial is contradicted by its own exception, and the exception points at a clause whose first limb excludes implants and whose second limb covers them. Reading excl.32's exception as empty makes the drafter write a pointless cross-reference; reading it as live makes '(Dental implants are not payable)' false. Retrieving only p35 answers a flat no. Retrieving only p16 answers yes up to the limit. Both are confidently wrong. Reference answer left for the author. Not yet read: whether the p16 limits table names implants."}
